                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Placement**\n\nI've been examining how the rule shifts red shapes within the grid, specifically focusing on example 3. My key observation is that the maximum x-coordinate of the red shapes in the input directly determines the rightmost position in the output. It seems the shift is about moving the shapes' distribution to the right edge.\n\n\n**Investigating Movement Dynamics**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into how individual shapes interact with this \"right wall\" effect. The question is: do connected components shift together, or does each shape undergo its own shift? Examining the test input reveals different maximum column values for separate shapes. This has led me to question whether this is a global grid shift or if each shape has its own transformation to the right. The thought process is now on gravity and how it relates to this.\n\n\n**Determining Object Shift**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on *how* shapes move, and it looks like a rigid body shift. I've been running through example 3, looking at row-by-row movements vs. connected component movement. It appears each shape moves as one unit, its shift distance determined by its rightmost point and the grid's right boundary. If I understand correctly, the shift seems to be independent of the initial position of the other objects.\n\n\n**Clarifying Rule Mechanics**\n\nI'm now clarifying the shift mechanism. Focusing on Example 3 and the test input, I'm trying to determine if it's a global shift or if each object moves independently to the right edge. I suspect independence, as in Example 3, each shape's rightmost pixel determines its shift. If so, Shape 1 and Shape 2 have max x = 3 each, so shift +6 each (to col 9). The test input supports this, with distinct shifts for Shape 1 (max x = 4) and Shape 2 (max x = 6).\n\n\n**Confirming \"Gravity\" Effect**\n\nI've made a crucial breakthrough! The concept is likely \"gravity to the right\" for each connected component. I was initially stuck because of the ambiguity in Example 3. The placement of objects in the row is the key. The lack of obstacles between objects and the right wall in the example is key. Now, I understand that each object \"falls\" to the right until it hits the boundary of the grid.\n\n\n**Refining the Object Movement**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on this object-centric shift, exploring Shape 1 and Shape 2 in this context. If \"gravity\" pulls each object to the right, as hypothesized, Shape 1, with a max x of 4, translates by 5 columns. I'm visualizing the shifts row by row. But I'm also challenging that with a global grid shift of 3 columns, which would result in an unusual floating position for Shape 1. This goes against my ARC intuition. Object-centric rules, with individual movement to a wall, are more common.\n\n\n",
